To solve this problem, we need to determine if it's possible to place n kings on the available cells of the chessboard without any of them attacking each other. The key insight is that a king can attack another king if they are adjacent in any of the four directions (up, down, left, right), including diagonally.

We can use a priority queue (min-heap) to always operate on the smallest remaining available cell first. For each query, we check if the available cells can form a valid placement of kings. If they can, we mark the unavailable cells as available. If they cannot, we return that the placement is not possible.

